# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository and checked out as LF
# everywhere, including Windows.
#
# Without this the repository held a mix: 84 of 774 tracked files carried CRLF
# while the rest carried LF, so any tool that rewrote a file whole - a formatter,
# sed, a generator - turned a one-line edit into a whole-file diff. Checking out
# as LF rather than native also stops `core.autocrlf` on a Windows clone from
# reintroducing the mix.
#
# The one thing that must not be LF is a shell script destined for a Linux
# runner, and `eol=lf` is exactly what guarantees that: .github/scripts/*.sh is
# invoked directly by CI, and a CR would make it fail with a bad interpreter.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception: cmd.exe is not reliable with LF-only batch
# files, and PowerShell scripts are conventionally CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Declared rather than left to detection, so a stray byte pattern can never make
# git treat one of these as text and rewrite it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.bm   binary
*.jks  binary
*.keystore binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.apk  binary
*.ipa  binary
*.dmg  binary
*.so   binary
*.dll  binary
*.dylib binary
